<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Utah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as University of Deseret</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> is a public research university in Salt Lake City. It was founded in 1850 by the General Assembly of the State of Deseret as the University of Deseret, closed for lack of funds after two years and reopened in 1867. It took its present name in 1892 and is now Utah's flagship university.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Soon after the Mormon pioneers arrived in the Salt Lake valley in 1847, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young</a> began organising a Board of Regents to establish a university. It was established on 28 February 1850 as the University of Deseret by the General Assembly of the provisional State of Deseret, with Orson Spencer as its first chancellor. Early classes were held in private homes or wherever space could be found. The university closed in 1853 because of a lack of funds and a lack of feeder schools.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After years of intermittent classes in the Salt Lake City Council House, the university began to be re-established in 1867 under David O. Calder, followed by John R. Park in 1869. It moved to the Union Academy building in 1876 and onto Union Square in 1884. In 1892 its name was changed to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>, four years before Utah became a state. Park then arranged to obtain land belonging to the army's Fort Douglas on the east bench of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-lake-valley">Salt Lake Valley</a>, where the university moved permanently in 1900. Park left his entire fortune to the university on his death in 1900.</p><p>Further Fort Douglas land was granted to the university over the years, and the fort was officially closed on 26 October 1991. The campus covers 1,534 acres, about 2 miles east of downtown Salt Lake City, close to the Wasatch Range, and includes the Health Sciences complex, Research Park and Fort Douglas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The university is governed by a Board of Trustees of ten members, eight of them appointed by the Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> with the consent of the Utah Senate. Together with the other public colleges of Utah it falls under the Utah Board of Higher Education. It organises its academic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and programmes into 17 colleges and schools. Its School of Medicine was Utah's first medical school.</p><p>The long-term genealogy work of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-jesus-christ-of-latter-day-saints">LDS Church</a> has allowed researchers to trace genetic disorders through several generations, and the university has contributed to the study of genetics. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Ballet was founded in 1951 by William F. Christensen. The athletic teams are called the Utes, and the men's basketball team won the NCAA title in 1944.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Utah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government organised irrigation, roads, a militia and the <b>University of Deseret</b>, the future University of Utah.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-deseret">State of Deseret</a></li></ul></section>
